    "font" : "CMR7",
    "font size" : 6.974,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "REVERSIBLE VECTOR FIELDS OF TYPE (2;1) 25"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 8,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.72, 627.629, 485.417, 674.579 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Therefore σ conjugates ϕ and Q. In particular it follows that Fix(Q) ⊂ Rm is sub vector space of dimension r. From (6) we know that Q is an involution. Thus from statements (i) and (ii) we have that there is a linear change of variables such that in these new variables Q is given by,"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 9,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.72, 593.309, 442.8, 620.432 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Q(x1,...,xm) = (x1,...,xr,−xr+1,...,−xm). The result now follows from the fact that ϕ is Ck-conjugated to Q by σ."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 10,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 204.481, 570.149, 407.388, 580.613 ],
    "font" : "CMCSC10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Appendix B. Poincar´e Compactification"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 11,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.721, 516.269, 485.409, 562.112 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Let X be a planar polynomial vector ﬁeld of degree n as our polynomial diﬀerential systems of Theorem 1. The Poincare´ compactiﬁed vector ﬁeld p(X) is an analytic vector ﬁeld on S2 constructed as follow (for more details see [17, Chapter 5])."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 12,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.721, 461.436, 485.452, 515.71 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "First we identify R2 with the plane (x1,x2,1) in R3 and deﬁne the Poincare´ sphere as S2 = {y = (y1,y2,y3) ∈ R3 : y12 + y22 + y32 = 1}. We deﬁne the northern hemisphere, the southern hemisphere and the equator respectively by H+ = {y ∈ S2 : y3 > 0}, H− = {y ∈ S2 : y3 < 0} and S1 = {y ∈ S2 : y3 = 0}."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 13,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 138.601, 449.436, 371.371, 467.83 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Consider now the projections f± : R2 → H± given by"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 14,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.719, 306.276, 485.424, 449.312 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "f±(x1,x2) = ±∆(x1,x2)(x1,x2,1), where ∆(x1,x2) = (x21 + x22 + 1)−21. These two maps deﬁne two copies of X, one copy X+ in H+ and one copy X− in H−. Consider the vector ﬁeld X′ = X+ ∪X− deﬁned in S2\\S1. Note that the inﬁnity of R2 is identiﬁed with the equator S1. The Poincar´e compactiﬁed vector ﬁeld p(X) is the analytic extension of X′ from S2\\S1 to S2 given by y3n−1X′. The Poincare´ disk D is the projection of the closed northern hemisphere to y3 = 0 under (y1,y2,y3)  → (y1,y2) (the vector ﬁeld given by this projection is also denoted by p(X)). Note that to know the behavior p(X) near S1 is the same than to know the behavior of X near the inﬁnity. We deﬁne the local charts of S2 by Ui = {y ∈ S2 : yi > 0} and Vi = {y ∈ S2 : yi < 0} for i ∈ {1,2,3}. In these charts we deﬁne φi : Ui → R2 and ψi : Vi → R2 by"
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 15,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 276.948, 428.868, 280.428, 429.228 ],
